        "day_number": 10,
        "title": "The Praise of a Godly Woman (Part 5) (2)",
        "content": "<p>Gen. 23. 2. Ioh. 11. 33, 35. 1 King. 14. 13. 1 Theſ. 4. 13. Eccleſ. 22. 11, 12 &amp; 38. 16, 17.</p><p>Act. 8. 2. 2 Chron. 32. 33.</p><p>Iam. 5. 10. Hebr. 12. 1. &amp; 13. 7. 1 Cor. 11. 1.</p><p>1 Sam. 2. 30. Deut. 26. 19. Ioh. 12. 26. Eſai. 8. 13. Timor Hominis, Dei Honor eſt. Tert. de Pœnit. c. 7</p><p>Tho. Aquin. 22. q. 103. a. 1. ad 3.</p><p>Pſal. 12. 6.</p><p>Pſal. 16. 3.</p><p>Pſal. 83. 3.</p><p>Mal. 3. 17.</p><p>2 Cor. 6. 18,</p><p>Cant. 4. 9.</p><p>Matth. 12. 50.</p><p>Hoſ. 2. 19.</p><p>Cant. 2. 10.</p><p>Cant. 2. 14.</p><p>Cant. 2. 13.</p><p>Cant. 6. 10.</p><p>Prou. 5. 19.</p><p>Ezek. 24. 16. Eccleſ. 36. 22.</p><p>Gen. 2. 18. Eccleſ. 36. 24.</p><p>Mal. 2. 14.</p><p>Eccleſ. 36. 24.</p><p>Prou. 31. 11.</p><p>Eccleſ. 26. 3, 23.</p><p>Prou. 18. 22.</p><p>Eccleſ. 26. 14.</p><p>Eccleſ. 26. 15.</p><p>Eccleſ. 26. 1.</p><p>Eccleſ. 26. 13.</p><p>Prou. 31. 23.</p><p>Eccleſ. 26. 22.</p><p>Prou. 19. 14.</p><p>Eccleſ. 40. 19.</p><p>Prou. 12. 4. Non annulus, non torques aureus, non monile, ſed Corona. Cartw. in Prou.</p><p>Pſal. 21. 3. Eſth. 2. 17</p><p>Eccleſ. 25. 10.</p><p>Eccleſ. 10. 24.</p><p>Eccleſ. 7. 19.</p><p>Prou. 31. 10.</p><p>Eccleſ. 26. 14. 15.</p><p>To Her Children ſhee ſhall be Commended; becauſe by her they haue a place of Refuge ; by Her they haue good meanes to bring and continue true Honour vpon them , and if They (the Fruit) be a great Bleſſing (as it is to haue Iſſue by ſuch a One) what is the Root that beareth it ?</p><p>But I muſt haſten: How ſhall ſhe be Praiſed in reſpect of her Parents? euen as Rachel for doing ſeruice to them as to her Maſters (the true propertie of one that feareth the Lord .) In reſpect of her kindred by mariage, as Ruth , louing them , Dealing kindly with them , and Cleauing vnto them . And in reſpect of her kindred by bloud, as Eſther , who did the Commandement of Mordecay when ſhe was a Queene, like as when ſhe was brought vp with Him , who was exceedingly grieued at his Griefe , and procured the Enlargement and Deliuerance of her kindred with her Feaſts , her Teares , and the Hazard of her Life .</p><p>In regard of her Seruants alſo, ſhe ſhall be commended becauſe ſhe Buildeth her Houſe : Shee is like the Merchants ſhip, She fetcheth her food from farre , She giueth meat to her Houſhold , She cloatheth them all with Scarlet , and Shee looketh ſo well to their waies , that As the Sunne when it ariſeth in the high Heauen; So is her beautie in the Ordering of her Houſe .</p><p>Laſtly, becauſe all the Eſſentiall Glory and Faireneſſe, which is to bee found in the whole Church, The Woman cloathed with the Sunne , as [ Pg 22 ] that of Iuſtification &amp; Sanctification &amp;c. belongs to euery Member of the Church , and cōſequently to euery Woman fearing the Lord , therefore to Gods people ſhe ſhall be commended, as one of the Hands of the Church dropping with ſweet ſmelling Mirrhe : as the Curtaines of Salomon ; As a Lilly among thornes ; A Garden incloſed: A Spring ſhut vp. A Fountain ſealed .</p><p>FOOTNOTES:</p><p>Eccleſ. 26. 1.</p><p>Eccleſ. 36. 23. Sine Muliere igitur Homo non habet Laudem, in Muliere prædicatur, &amp;c. S. Ambros. Inſtit. Virg. cap. 3. tom. 1.</p><p>Prou. 14. 26. &amp; 11. 22.</p><p>Deut. 5. 29. Pſal. 112. 2.</p><p>Pſal. 127. 3.</p><p>Pſal. 128. 3.</p><p>Gen. 29. 9.</p><p>Eccleſ. 3. 7.</p><p>Ruth. 4. 15.</p><p>Ruth. 1. 8.</p><p>Ruth. 1. 14. &amp; c. 2. 11</p><p>Eſth. 2. 20.</p><p>Eſth. 4. 4.</p><p>Eſth. 4. 16.</p><p>Eſth. 8. 3.</p><p>Eſth. 4. 11, 16.</p><p>Prou. 14. 1.</p><p>Prou. 31. 14.</p><p>Prou. 31. 15.</p><p>Prou. 31. 21.</p><p>Prou. 31. 27.</p><p>Eccleſ. 26. 16.</p><p>Reuel. 12. 1.</p><p>But when ſhall ſhee haue all this Praiſe and of whom? Not by and by, nor of Euery one , for Praiſe is not comely in the mouth of euery one, of euery ſcoffing Iſhmael .</p><p>But firſt of the Time.</p><p>Many when they heare a Promiſe (and a Promiſe I told you at firſt, this is) thinke to haue it by and by; but they marke not, that a Promiſe and the Fruition of it is not all at once. It muſt be waited for , eſpecially this Promiſe of Praiſe: Vntill the Lord come (as the Apoſtles limit the Time) and then , at his Appearing, ſhe ſhall haue praiſe of God . Then at his Appearing , Her Faith ſhall be found vnto Praiſe and Honour, and Glory . Therefore in the mean while, beſt for her to Feare the Lord , and ſo be praiſe-worthy, than to be praiſed for the preſent.</p><p>1. Not only becauſe it is ſafeſt praiſing Her as a Maſter of a ſhip is (ſaith S. Ambroſe ) when ſhe [ Pg 23 ] is ſafely arriued in the Hauen, paſt all danger of ſhipwracke: or growing more proud by her praiſe (which many Liuing doe) as Herod for one , and that Philoſopher for another, whoſe ſoule being before no bigger than a mans finger, became ſo puft vp and ſwolne with others commending him, that (as Arrianus reports) it grew greater than two Cubits .</p><p>FOOTNOTES:</p><p>Gal. 3. 28. Epheſ. 4. 15.16. Cant. 2. 10.—Cum ipſos cogitatis amantes, non virum &amp; Fœminam, ſed verbum &amp;c. Animam ſentiatis, oportet. Et ſi Chriſtum &amp; Eccleſiam dixero, idem eſt, niſi quod Eccleſiæ nomine non vna Anima, ſed multarum vnitas, vel potius vnanimitas deſignatur. S. Bern. ſerm. 61. in Cant.</p><p>Cant. 5. 5.</p><p>Cant. 1. 5.</p><p>Cant. 2. 2.</p><p>Cant. 4. 12.</p><p>Luk. 6. 26. Eccleſ. 15. 9.</p>",
